117. If a man does evil, let him not do it again and again;
let him not find delight in it; suffering is the accumulation of evil.

118. If a man does good, let him do it again and again;
let him find delight in it; happiness is the accumulation of good.

119. Even the evil-doer sees good, as long as his evil deed has not ripened;
but when his evil deed ripens, then the evil-doer sees evil.

120. Even the good man sees evil, as long as his good deed has not ripened;
but when his good deed ripens, then the good man sees good.

121. Let no one think lightly of evil, saying: "it will not come near me";
for by the falling of drops even a water-jar is filled;
the fool becomes full of evil, even if he gathers it little by little.

122. Let no one think lightly of good, saying: "it will not come near me";
for by the falling of drops even a water-jar is filled;
the wise man becomes full of good, even if he gathers it little by little.

123. As a merchant with a small escort and great wealth avoids a dangerous road,
as one who loves life avoids poison, so should one avoid evil deeds.

